[158.171593, "o", "cat /app/output/ANALYSIS_SUMMARY.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[158.172871, "o", "# E-Commerce Anomaly Analysis: March 2020 Pandemic Impact\r\n\r\n## Executive Summary\r\n\r\nThis analysis identified anomalous daily sales patterns in product categories during March 2020 (COVID-19 pandemic onset) compared to the baseline period of January-February 2020 and historical 2019 data.\r\n\r\n## Data Overview\r\n\r\n- **Survey Respondents**: 5,027 unique customers\r\n- **Purchase Transactions**: 438,050 cleaned transactions (2019-2020)\r\n- **Product Categories Analyzed**: 243 unique categories\r\n- **Demographic Features Engineered**: 41 features\r\n- **Analysis Period**: \r\n  - Baseline: January 1 - February 29, 2020\r\n  - Treatment: March 1 - March 31, 2020\r\n  - Historical: Full year 2019\r\n\r\n## Key Findings\r\n\r\n### TOP 10 SURGE CATEGORIES (Anomaly Index: +74.98 to +25.96)\r\n\r\n1. **PROTECTIVE_GLOVE** (Index: 74.98)\r\n   - Treatment avg spend: $0.84 vs Baseline: $0.28\r\n   - Purchase rate increase: 1.95% → 3.85%\r\n   - Top Intensive Driver: Non-smokers (+$0.69 DID)\r\n   - Top Extensive Driver: Age 55-64 demographic (+0.015 purchase probability)\r\n\r\n2. **MEDICATION** (Index: 46.35)\r\n   - Treatment avg spend: $168.91 vs Baseline: $3.63\r\n   - Clear pandemic-driven health concern purchases\r\n\r\n3. **SKIN_CLEANING_WIPE** (Index: 40.39)\r\n   - Treatment avg spend: $82.23 vs Baseline: $2.04\r\n   - Hygiene product surge\r\n\r\n4. **TOILET_PAPER** (Index: 34.82)\r\n   - Treatment avg spend: $110.91 vs Baseline: $3.18\r\n   - Classic pandemic hoarding behavior\r\n\r\n5. **CLEANING_AGENT** (Index: 31.24)\r\n   - Treatment avg spend: $91.97 vs Baseline: $2.94\r\n   - Disinfection focus\r\n\r\n### TOP 10 SLUMP CATEGORIES (Anomaly Index: -12.16 to -9.17)\r\n\r\n1. **MEAL_HOLDER** (Index: -12.16)\r\n   - Treatment avg spend: $0.07 vs Baseline: $0.30\r\n   - Purchase rate drop: 1.92% → 0.48%\r\n   - Top Intensive Driver: Age 18-24 (-$1.25 DID, highly significant p<0.0001)\r\n   - Top Extensive Driver: Age 18-24 (-0.026 purchase probability, p<0.0001)\r\n\r\n2. **PLANNER** (Index: -11.99)\r\n   - Lifestyle/office product decline\r\n\r\n3. **PERSONAL_FRAGRANCE** (Index: -11.24)\r\n   - Luxury/non-essential item reduction\r\n\r\n4. **WATCH** (Index: -10.66)\r\n   - Discretionary spending pullback\r\n\r\n## Demographic Drivers of Anomalies\r\n\r\n### Intensive Margin (Total Spending)\r\n\r\n**Surge Categories** - Top Drivers:\r\n- Non-smokers and non-drinkers increased spending on protective/health items\r\n- Health-conscious demographics drove premium spending\r\n- Substance use status emerged as significant predictor\r\n\r\n**Slump Categories** - Top Drivers:\r\n- Younger demographics (18-24) significantly reduced spending on lifestyle items\r\n- Age-related effects were highly significant (p<0.0001)\r\n- Educational attainment showed modest effects\r\n\r\n### Extensive Margin (Purchase Probability)\r\n\r\n**Surge Categories** - Top Drivers:\r\n- Age 55-64 demographic more likely to purchase protective items\r\n- High-income households increased purchase frequency\r\n- Older demographics (55+) showed protective behavior\r\n\r\n**Slump Categories** - Top Drivers:\r\n- Young adults (18-24) dramatically reduced purchase probability\r\n- Hispanic demographic showed reduced engagement\r\n- Younger age groups most affected by lifestyle product pullback\r\n\r\n## Causal Insights\r\n\r\n### Pandemic-Related Behavioral Shifts\r\n\r\n1. **Health & Safety Focus**: Clear surge in protective equipment, medication, and cleaning products\r\n   - Driven by health-conscious, older demographics\r\n   - Non-smokers/non-drinkers showed higher engagement (health-aware segment)\r\n\r\n2. **Lifestyle Pullback**: Significant decline in discretionary, lifestyle, and fashion items\r\n   - Predominantly affecting younger demographics (18-24)\r\n   - May reflect income uncertainty or lockdown-related lifestyle changes\r\n\r\n3. **Demographic Divergence**:\r\n   - Older (55+): Increased protective purchases, health focus\r\n   - Younger (18-24): Reduced lifestyle spending, pullback in discretionary items\r\n   - Income effect: High-income households maintained or increased spending\r\n\r\n## Statistical Methodology\r\n\r\n- **Anomaly Detection**: Z-score based deviation index (-100 to +100 scale)\r\n- **Causal Analysis**: Difference-in-Differences (DiD) estimation\r\n  - Intensive Margin: Univariate DiD on total spending\r\n  - Extensive Margin: Multivariate heterogeneous DiD on purchase probability\r\n- **Features Analyzed**: 41 demographic and behavioral features\r\n"]
[158.17301, "o", "- **Sample Size**: Top 20 categories × 2,283-4,500 users per category\r\n\r\n## Output Files Generated\r\n\r\n1. `survey_cleaned.csv` - Cleaned demographic data (5,027 respondents)\r\n2. `amazon-purchases-2019-2020-filtered.csv` - Cleaned transaction data (438,050 transactions)\r\n3. `category_anomaly_index.csv` - Anomaly scores for 243 product categories\r\n4. `survey_feature_engineered.csv` - 41 engineered demographic features\r\n5. `user_category_period_aggregated_intensive.csv` - Spending data for DiD analysis\r\n6. `user_category_period_aggregated_extensive.csv` - Purchase indicator data for DiD analysis\r\n7. `causal_analysis_report.json` - Complete DiD results with top 3 drivers per margin\r\n\r\n## Conclusion\r\n\r\nThe March 2020 pandemic onset created stark divergence in consumer behavior:\r\n- **Health-conscious, older demographics** increased protective/health product spending\r\n- **Younger demographics** dramatically reduced discretionary/lifestyle spending\r\n- **Non-smokers/non-drinkers** showed stronger engagement with health products\r\n- The effects were statistically significant across both intensive (spending) and extensive (participation) margins\r\n\r\n"]
